.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt PBCOPY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pbcopy ,
.Nm pbpaste
.Nd copy to and paste from the pasteboard
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm pbcopy
.Op Fl help
.Op Fl pboard Ar general | ruler | find | font
.Nm pbpaste
.Op Fl help
.Op Fl pboard Ar general | ruler | find | font
.Op Fl Prefer Ar txt | rtf | ps
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm pbcopy
reads its standard input to the end and puts it on a pasteboard,
replacing what was there.
Input that starts as an RTF document
.Pq Ql {\ertf
goes in as RTF; anything else as plain text, which is taken to be UTF-8.
.Pp
.Nm pbpaste
writes a pasteboard's plain text to its standard output.
.Bl -tag -width "-pboard name"
.It Fl pboard Ar name
The pasteboard to use:
.Cm general
(the default, the one copy and paste use),
.Cm ruler ,
.Cm find
or
.Cm font .
.It Fl Prefer Ar type
For
.Nm pbpaste :
the type to write when the pasteboard has it,
.Cm rtf
or
.Cm ps
(PostScript), else its plain text;
.Cm txt ,
the default, is plain text.
.It Fl help
Prints the usage and exits.
.El
.Pp
The pasteboards belong to the user running the command: other users,
.Xr sudo 8
included, do not see them.
They are kept by the pasteboard server,
.Pa /usr/libexec/pboard ,
which
.Xr launchd 8
starts on demand, and are lost when it stops.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std pbcopy
.Nm pbpaste
exits 0 also when the pasteboard holds nothing it can write.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a file's text, and paste it into another:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pbcopy < notes.txt
pbpaste > copy.txt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr launchd 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm pbcopy
and
.Nm pbpaste
first appeared in Minkar 19.6.
